        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new `formatAgeBand` lands exactly where its siblings already live. The `OwnerMapper` interface is the established home for a family of `default` derive-and-format methods (`formatMembershipNumber`, `formatCheckDigit`, `formatContactPreference`, etc.), each wired into `toOwnerDto` via a matching `@Mapping(... expression = \"java(formatX(owner))\")` line. The addition follows that pattern faithfully:\n\n- The `@Mapping` entry is added alongside the others, in the same style.\n- The method mirrors the null-guard idiom of `formatMembershipNumber`/`formatCheckDigit` (guarding owner plus the required dependent fields, returning `null` when the band can't be derived) rather than inventing a new convention.\n- The Javadoc matches the thoroughness and tone of the surrounding methods.\n- Nothing is duplicated, and the interface isn't becoming a catch-all \u2014 derivation logic for the DTO is precisely what this mapper is for, so this is additive rather than piling an unrelated concern onto the class.\n\nThe `Owner` field/getter/setter/`toString` additions follow the existing member ordering and conventions, and the schema and OpenAPI changes are placed consistently next to `registration_date`/`registrationDate` across all four dialects and both DTO definitions.\n\nOne trivial, non-blocking nit (not worth a change request on its own): `java.time.Period` is referenced fully-qualified inline, whereas the rest of the codebase imports its types. A maintainer would likely prefer `import java.time.Period;`, but this is a stylistic micro-detail and doesn't affect where the logic lives or how coherent the structure is."
      }
